      "updated_at": "2026-03-17T15:18:33Z",
      "body": "I'm not entirely sure if `signaldict` holding a strong reference is the correct solution. The reason why `signaldict` currently holds a borrowed reference is because it should live as long as the `Context` object. Therefore, I think that the best solution would be something that enforces this lifetime.\n\nOne potential solution would be to change `context_clear` to this:\n```c\nstatic int\ncontext_clear(PyObject *op)\n{\n    PyDecContextObject *self = _PyDecContextObject_CAST(op);\n    /* Start of added code */\n    PyDecSignalDictObject *traps = _PyDecSignalDictObject_CAST(self->traps);\n    PyDecSignalDictObject *flags = _PyDecSignalDictObject_CAST(self->flags);\n    traps->flags = NULL;\n    flags->flags = NULL;\n    /* End of added code */\n    Py_CLEAR(self->traps);\n    Py_CLEAR(self->flags);\n    return 0;\n}\n\n```\n\nas this would result in a `ValueError` (invalid signal dict) if you try to call `repr`. Raising an exception seems to be the best strategy as this is a state you would never want to be in.\n\nCan open a PR if this is an acceptable fix.",
